{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "212",
    "case_summary": "An 18-year-old Chinese woman noted a grey dot in the central vision of her left eye 4 days before presentation. She had a known plexiform neurofibroma of the arm confirmed by biopsy 1 year earlier; otherwise, she denied medical history, trauma, family history, smoking, coffee, tea, illicit drugs, or oral contraceptive use. Blood pressure was 110/70 mmHg. Visual acuity was 20/20 in the right eye and 20/25 in the left eye, with no relative afferent pupillary defect. Slit-lamp examination showed mild subcapsular cataracts bilaterally. Automated perimetry revealed a small central scotoma in the left eye. Fundus examination demonstrated a swollen optic disc in the right eye and, in the left eye, a parafoveal grey-white deep retinal lesion and a cotton-wool spot in the posterior pole. Fluorescein angiography showed a focal retinal filling defect corresponding to the cotton-wool spot and hypofluorescence without leakage at the right optic disc. Swept-source optical coherence tomography of the left macula revealed hyperreflective bands in the inner nuclear layer consistent with paracentral acute middle maculopathy. Carotid Doppler ultrasound and transthoracic echocardiography were normal. Laboratory studies, including complete blood count with differential, liver enzymes, renal function, lipid profile, erythrocyte sedimentation rate, C-reactive protein, blood glucose, hemoglobin A1C, antinuclear antibody, anti–double-stranded DNA antibody, anti–neutrophil cytoplasmic antibody, syphilis serology, HIV, tuberculosis, toxoplasmosis, angiotensin-converting enzyme, and tests for protein C, protein S, antithrombin III, and homocystinuria, were unremarkable. Magnetic resonance angiography of the brain was normal. Brain MRI with and without contrast showed bilateral vestibular schwannomas, a parasellar mass consistent with a meningioma, and a cervical extramedullary lesion. Orbital CT showed normal optic nerve contours and calcified lesions in the right temporal lobe.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Neurofibromatosis type 2\nB. Thrombophilia\nC. Autoimmune vasculitis\nD. Infectious causes\nE. Embolic sources from carotid artery disease\nF. Cardiac embolic sources\nG. Moyamoya syndrome\nH. Drug\n",
